== rm sees a dangling symlink, so it would remove it, not report it absent:
rm: would remove 'link'
== --dry-run prints without deleting a file:
rm: would remove 'keep.txt'
keep.txt still present
== rm -i honors both answers under --dry-run:
declined=empty
accepted=rm: would remove 'keep.txt'
empty-line-declines=empty
== --dry-run -r lists a tree in removal order without deleting it:
rm: would remove 'tree/sub/b'
rm: would remove 'tree/sub'
rm: would remove 'tree'
tree still present
== rm refuses '.' and the root directory, before any removal:
-c:1:1: error: Koshkit `rm`: Cannot remove '.': Is a directory.
     1 |  koshkit rm --dry-run .
       |  ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
-c:1:1: error: Koshkit `rm`: Cannot remove '/': Is a directory.
     1 |  koshkit rm --dry-run /
       |  ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
== -x and --one-file-system change nothing inside one file system:
== rm -rf . is refused (count of the refusal):
1
== rm -rf .. is refused (count):
1
== the directory contents survive:
keep-survives
== rm -rf / is refused (count of the refusal):
1
== another spelling of the root is refused (count):
1
== a drive or root spelled with a trailing separator is refused (count):
1
== rm without -r reports a directory instead of refusing it:
2
== a normal file is reported for removal under --dry-run, and survives:
rm: would remove 'keep'
keep-survives
== rm -I asks once for more than three operands:
kosh: Remove 4 arguments? [y/N] status=0

== rm -I asks once for a recursive removal:
kosh: Remove 1 argument recursively? [y/N] status=0

== rm -i states the file type in its prompt:
kosh: Descend into directory 'tree'
 [y/N] status=0
kosh: Descend into directory 'single'
 [y/N] kosh: Remove regular file 'single/full'
 [y/N] kosh: Remove directory 'single'
 [y/N] -c:1:1: error: Koshkit `rm`: Cannot remove 'single': Directory not empty.
     1 |  koshkit rm -ri --dry-run single; echo status=$

       |  ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
status=1
== the last of -f and -i decides:
rm: would remove 'a'
